<h3>What this unit is about</h3>
<p>The sun has risen every morning of your life. Every morning before your life. Every morning anyone has ever recorded. So it will rise tomorrow. Right?</p>
<p>Probably. But David Hume wants to know: what actually <em>justifies</em> that conclusion? You have seen the past. You have not seen the future. The fact that something has always happened before is not, strictly speaking, a logical guarantee that it will happen again. The future is not the past. So on what basis do we feel so confident?</p>
<p>This is not a trick question. It is the heart of Hume’s philosophy — and the more you think about it, the stranger it gets. Hume’s great contribution is to take this strangeness seriously and follow it to its conclusions, however unsettling those conclusions turn out to be.</p>
<h4>The shape of the argument</h4>
<p>Hume begins from a position called <strong>empiricism</strong>: the view that all genuine knowledge is ultimately grounded in experience. We have two kinds of mental content, he says — <em>impressions</em>, which are the direct deliverances of the senses and emotions, and <em>ideas</em>, which are the mind’s fainter copies of those impressions. Anything that cannot be traced back to an original impression is philosophically suspect.</p>
<p>That standard does serious damage to things philosophers have long taken for granted. Take causation. When you see one billiard ball strike another and the second ball move, you feel certain that the first ball <em>caused</em> the second to move. But what exactly did you <em>see</em>? You saw one event followed by another. You did not see any force, any power, any necessary connection between them. You saw succession. And from repeated successions, your mind formed a habit of expectation — nothing more.</p>
<p>This is Hume’s skepticism: not that we can’t know anything (he is quite happy to rely on experience as a practical guide), but that the foundations of our most confident beliefs are much shakier than we thought. Reason, which Descartes had vindicated as the instrument of certain knowledge, turns out to have very limited reach. The things we know best — that the sun will rise, that fire will burn, that the future will resemble the past — are not products of reason at all. They are habits of mind.</p>
<div class="callout callout--concept"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">💡</span><div class="callout__content">
<p>Why it matters</p>
<p>Hume’s challenge to causation and induction goes to the heart of how science works. Scientists observe patterns and infer laws. Hume asks: can that inference ever be fully justified? His answer — that it cannot be justified by reason alone, only by habit and custom — is one of the most consequential arguments in the history of philosophy. It famously woke Immanuel Kant from what he called his “dogmatic slumber” and set the agenda for philosophy in the century that followed.</p>
</div></div>
<div>
<p>← Looking back &nbsp;|&nbsp; Looking ahead →</p>
<p>In Unit 2, Descartes set out to secure knowledge on a foundation of rational certainty. He doubted everything he could doubt, found the one thing he couldn’t — the fact of his own thinking — and rebuilt from there, with God as guarantor of reason’s reliability. Hume’s empiricism is the counter-attack: if all knowledge must ultimately trace back to experience, then reason alone — without sensory grounding — tells us almost nothing. The certainty Descartes thought he had secured turns out to rest on habit.</p>
<p>In Unit 4, Hegel will respond to the problem Hume opened up. Where Hume concludes that reason cannot reach beyond experience, Hegel will argue that reason does not merely describe reality — it <em>constitutes</em> it. If Hume empties reason out, Hegel tries to fill it back in on an entirely different basis.</p>
</div>
