<h3>Unit reading</h3>
<p>This unit has one primary text, assigned in five sections. The <em>Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding</em> is Hume’s own condensed and clarified version of his earlier, longer <em>Treatise of Human Nature</em>. It is deliberately written for a general audience and is, by the standards of this course, quite readable. Plan to read each section carefully — they are short but dense.</p>
<h4>Primary text</h4>
<div>
<p>David Hume, <em>Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding</em></p>
<p>Trans. Jonathan Bennett, <em>Early Modern Texts</em>, 2017 (modernized English)</p>
<p><strong>Assigned:</strong> Sections 1–5 (approximately 35 pages)</p>
<p>Available free online: earlymoderntexts.com/assets/pdfs/hume1748.pdf</p>
</div>
<h4>Reading guide</h4>
<p><strong>Section 1</strong> — “Different Species of Philosophy.” Hume distinguishes the kind of philosophy he is doing — rigorous, empirically grounded — from what he sees as the vague, speculative variety. Short but important for orientation.</p>
<p><strong>Section 2</strong> — “Origin of Ideas.” Introduces the impressions/ideas distinction and the Copy Principle. This is the empiricist foundation everything else rests on.</p>
<p><strong>Section 3</strong> — “Association of Ideas.” Three principles explain how the mind connects ideas: resemblance, contiguity, and cause and effect. Short; read it carefully.</p>
<p><strong>Section 4</strong> — “Sceptical Doubts.” The most important section. Hume introduces his famous fork dividing all knowledge into “relations of ideas” and “matters of fact,” then argues that our belief in cause and effect cannot be justified a priori. Read it twice if you can.</p>
<p><strong>Section 5</strong> — “Sceptical Solution.” Hume’s answer to his own challenge: if reason can’t justify our causal inferences, what does? Custom and habit. This section also contains his account of belief.</p>
<div class="callout callout--concept"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">💡</span><div class="callout__content">
<p>Reading tip</p>
<p>Hume sometimes writes in a voice of confident, almost breezy skepticism — as though the unsettling conclusions he is reaching are perfectly natural. Don’t let that tone make it feel easy. Pause when something seems obvious and ask: <em>is it actually obvious, or does Hume have a point?</em></p>
</div></div>
